The current zip includes **the full native Postiz dashboard and its analytics UI**, because it runs the complete Postiz application rather than just my Python wrapper. Upstream explicitly says the self-hosted version currently has the same feature set as hosted, including scheduling/calendar, analytics, team management and media library. ([GitHub][1])

But I would **not describe the zip as “all analytics done.”** There are three distinct layers:

```text
L1  Postiz dashboard              INCLUDED
    human-facing operational UI

L2  Postiz analytics APIs         INCLUDED
    programmatic channel + post metrics

L3  Our media-network intelligence dashboard
    NOT BUILT YET
```

And **L3 is ultimately the interesting part for your system**.

## What you already get visually

Once the Docker stack is running at your Postiz URL, this isn't a headless service. You get the Postiz frontend with its channels, calendar/scheduling, media management and analytics screens. Postiz's frontend and backend are part of the same upstream application deployed by the image in the zip. ([GitHub][2])

So conceptually:

```text
POSTIZ

Dashboard
│
├── Calendar
│   ├── scheduled posts
│   ├── drafts
│   └── published posts
│
├── Channels
│   ├── YouTube A
│   ├── TikTok A
│   ├── Instagram A
│   ├── YouTube B
│   └── ...
│
├── Media library
│
├── Analytics
│   ├── channel/platform analytics
│   └── individual post analytics
│
├── Team
│
└── Developer/API
```

Postiz's public API exposes the same fundamental analytics primitives programmatically. There is a platform/integration analytics endpoint and a separate post analytics endpoint. Both return metric series and percentage changes, with actual metrics depending on what the social platform exposes. ([Postiz Documentation][3])

### Channel analytics

For each of your 15 integrations:

```http
GET /public/v1/analytics/{integration_id}?date=30
```

can return structures such as:

```json
[
  {
    "label": "Followers",
    "data": [
      {"total": "1250", "date": "2026-08-01"},
      {"total": "1280", "date": "2026-08-02"}
    ],
    "percentageChange": 2.4
  }
]
```

The important detail is that the path uses the **actual Postiz integration ID**, not `"youtube"` or a display name. ([Postiz Documentation][4])

### Post analytics

For an individual published object:

```http
GET /public/v1/analytics/post/{post_id}?date=30
```

Postiz exposes the analogous post-level series—likes, comments, shares, impressions and whatever else that provider supports. ([Postiz Documentation][3])

