# What my zip adds

The zip has a small client already wired to both:

```bash
python3 tools/postiz_client.py \
  analytics-integration INTEGRATION_ID \
  --days 30
```

and:

```bash
python3 tools/postiz_client.py \
  analytics-post POST_ID \
  --days 30
```

It also contains:

```text
tools/snapshot_analytics.py
```

which loops over all configured brands/accounts:

```text
prehistoric
 ├─ youtube
 ├─ instagram
 └─ tiktok

roboblades
 ├─ youtube
 ├─ instagram
 └─ tiktok

...
```

and appends their raw account analytics to:

```text
data/analytics.jsonl
```

in a structure roughly like:

```json
{
  "observed_at": "...",
  "brand": "prehistoric",
  "platform": "youtube",
  "integration_id": "...",
  "ok": true,
  "raw": [...]
}
```

I deliberately preserved the raw payload instead of trying to pretend that an Instagram `view`, TikTok `view`, and YouTube `view` mean precisely the same thing.

That decision becomes important once we start doing statistical comparisons.

